    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\n`j`/`k` and `gj`/`gk` share the same internal goal column (`curswant`). For `j`/`k` that's correct: it's a buffer-column concept, and it should survive short or empty lines so you land back at your intended column once lines get longer again.\n\n`gj`/`gk` are display-line motions though, so their contract is different: move to the screen row physically above/below the current one. On a genuinely empty line there's only one possible screen column, 1, but landing there via a vertical motion doesn't touch `curswant` (correct for `j`/`k`). If `gk`/`gj` is then pressed from that empty line, it uses whatever unrelated `curswant` was left over from an earlier horizontal motion instead of the empty line's own, unambiguous column.\n\nRepro with `nvim -u NONE`:\n\n1. Buffer with a short line, then a long line that wraps across several screen rows (narrow window), then an empty line, then another line.\n2. On the long line, press `$` (sets curswant to \"end of line\").\n3. `j` onto the empty line below. Cursor column becomes 1, curswant stays at \"end of line\".\n4. `gk`.\n\nCursor jumps to the true end of the long line, wherever that lands on the wrapped display, instead of the display row immediately above the empty line.\n\nThis is specific to empty lines, not short lines in general. A short line that's part of the same wrapped paragraph is a genuine word-wrap artifact, and keeping curswant through it is desirable so a later `gj`/`gk` can return to the wider column. An empty line isn't a continuation of anything though, its screen column is unambiguous, and using a stale buffer-column there produces motion with no relationship to what's on screen.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n`gj`/`gk` track their own desired screen column separately from `j`/`k`'s `curswant`, or at least treat a zero-length line as setting the current screen column to 1 for the purposes of the next `gj`/`gk`. Leaving an empty line with `gk` should go to the row directly above it.\n\nEnvironment: Neovim v0.12.4, reproduced with `nvim -u NONE` (not config-related).",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
