            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Zooming out a bit: this is old, inherited Vim behavior, not an Nvim regression, and I get there's real weight in staying compatible with how `j`/`k` have always worked. `curswant` persisting through short and empty lines is a genuinely useful feature for those two.\n\nWhat I think is an actual gap though: `gj`/`gk` don't have a screen-relative equivalent of that concept at all, they just reuse `j`/`k`'s buffer-column `curswant` outright. So the `g`-prefixed motions promise screen-relative movement but don't fully deliver on it for column memory, at least on empty lines where the discrepancy is visible. That's not something I expected going in, it reads more like an implementation gap than a deliberate design choice, since I can't find anything that depends on `gj`/`gk` sharing `curswant` with `j`/`k`.\n\nWhether it's worth a change is a call on priority and vi-compatibility that's really yours to make. Happy to test anything if it goes somewhere.",
