            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> What I think is an actual gap though: `gj`/`gk` don't have a screen-relative equivalent of that concept at all, they just reuse `j`/`k`'s buffer-column `curswant` outright. So the `g`-prefixed motions promise screen-relative movement but don't fully deliver on it for column memory, at least on empty lines where the discrepancy is visible. That's not something I expected going in, it reads more like an implementation gap than a deliberate design choice, since I can't find anything that depends on `gj`/`gk` sharing `curswant` with `j`/`k`.\n\nThis isn't exactly the case.\n\n```\n-- Single wrapped physical line\nfoo bar bazz buzz fo\no bar BAZZ buzz\n\nfoo bar\n```\n\nIf you do `G0g_2gk`, the cursor will end up at the beginning of BAZZ like you would expect, since `g_` doesn't snap to the end of line like `$` does. So even though they pull from the same `curswant` variable, the screenwise version does still transform it into something that makes sense.\n\nIn this case, the `nv_screengo` function (`normal.c` line `2481`) specifically resets curswant to MAX_COL if it started there. This would make sense since it's how `$` behaves in Vim/Nvim in every other context that I can think of.\n\nIntroducing this change would also make a linewise motion decrease curswant without `startofline` being true, which as far as I'm aware would be a new behavior (`_` being an odd case, since it's seen by operators as linewise, but the destination is charwise). This creates questions about if `G0$gkgk` behaves differently from `G0$2gk`, since the former passes through the blank line but the latter skips it (at least from the user's perspective).\n\nGiven how common remapping `j`/`k` to `gj`/`gk` on count1 is, this might introduce a lot of confusion.",
